{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel and say to them: You shall be holy.</b> He specified 'all the congregation,' for an עֵדָה is no fewer than ten, and nothing pertaining to sanctity is [done] with fewer than ten. Therefore he said here, 'Speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel' — to every single congregation, that they should be holy through the fact that they are permitted to every matter of sanctity in which they sanctify His name, blessed be He. This is what he said: 'for holy am I.'" "And he prefaced this portion with the matter of Israel's sanctity, for just as it is written before the giving of the Torah, 'And you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation,' so too in this portion all Ten Commandments are alluded to, as Rabbi Levi said (see Leviticus Rabbah 24:5). Therefore he prefaced with sanctity in order to endear the commandments upon them, to say that through them they would merit the rank of sanctity. And do not say that sanctity is a trifling matter — 'for holy am I': is it a small thing in your eyes to liken the form to its Maker, as it were? And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Leviticus Rabbah 24:6): wherever you find a fence around illicit relations, there you find sanctity. Now you already know that the term 'fence' refers to a thing that is permitted by law, and they make a safeguard and a fence to distance themselves from the prohibition. Therefore they said that wherever you find a fence around illicit relations, there you find sanctity — for all sanctity is precisely in that which is permitted, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sifrei Re'eh 122): 'Sanctify yourself in what is permitted to you.' And the sanctity of the nazirite proves this. Therefore they said: in the place where a fence around illicit relations is mentioned, you find sanctity. And so too at the giving of the Torah: 'Do not approach a woman' — that is, [regarding women] permitted to them; therefore He called them 'a holy nation.' And so too here it is stated: 'You shall not approach to uncover nakedness' — an approach that leads to illicit relations, and this is a fence and a safeguard against illicit relations. And likewise a zona and a ḥalala — [these involve] things permitted to an Israelite, and the priest needs a fence and a safeguard to forbid even that which is permitted to an Israelite, in order to sanctify him with extra sanctity." "And what He said, 'You shall be holy,' carries in its meaning both the sense of a command and the sense of informing of the matter — just as 'And you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation' is an informing of the matter. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, informed them of the reward for the fences around illicit relations, that through this they would be holy to their Elohim."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:24.782757419Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}